{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Thus says YHWH: At about midnight.</b> The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him 'at midnight,' for thus the Torah testified in the event: ״ויהי בחצי הלילה וה׳ הכה כל בכור״ — 'and it came to pass at midnight, and YHWH smote every firstborn.' But Moses said כחצות — 'about midnight.' Our Rabbis of blessed memory explained: in order that the astrologers of Pharaoh not err and say, 'Moses is a liar.'" "<b>And you should be roused to ask</b> about this teaching: for surely the astrologers of Pharaoh and his sorcerers, from the third plague onward — when they acknowledged against their will and said ״אצבע אלהים היא״ ('it is the finger of Elohim') — did not appear before Moses nor came into the king's palace at all, for they saw in Moses that he turned all their wisdom backward and made their knowledge foolish. If so, how could they pin the error on Moses and say 'Moses is a liar' — they who knew that all his words were trustworthy through the nine preceding plagues, and not one of his words fell to the ground? And behold, they were wise men of the wise, who recognized that Moses' deeds were from HaShem, for they had already acknowledged as much. And furthermore: even if they were to pin the error on Moses, why would Moses be concerned about them, that he should change on their account the expression that was said to him from the mouth of the Almighty — בחצות ('at midnight') — and say to them in His name כחצות ('about midnight')?" "<b>And it appears</b> proper to say that until the third plague, the astrologers and sorcerers were firm in their belief, and the matter was clear to them: that all the deeds of Moses our master were by way of wisdom and stratagem, not from HaShem. But when the third plague came, they contradicted their own wisdom and acknowledged Moses' prophecy, and then they said ״אצבע אלהים היא.״ And from then on, they saw that the plagues came at Moses's word and immediately after his warning, and they saw that they departed each and every time through the power of his prayer. Then the matter went on growing stronger in their hearts, and Moses's prophecy became verified among them, and the Name of Heaven became known and sanctified in the world through him. And since his words had proved trustworthy through all the plagues, and the standing of the sorcerers and astrologers kept declining through their lies and impudence, while the truth of Moses's prophecy grew ever stronger — therefore Moses feared, in this last plague, which is the seal of the ten plagues, lest the sorcerers and astrologers find an opening for error in his words such as they had not found until now among the preceding plagues. For if they were now to find an opening for error, they would deny all the plagues and signs and wonders retroactively, and the Name of Heaven would be profaned. Therefore the matter was compelling for Moses, that he needed to change the expression of HaShem, lest they err and deny retroactively, and the Name of Heaven be profaned. And he was concerned about this in the last plague because all the preceding signs depend upon it." "<b>And it appears</b> to me further, regarding what he changed and said כחצות ('about midnight'), that he needed to do so in order to sanctify the Name of Heaven. For it is well known among the scholars of the stars that the heads of the days, which are חל״ם כצנ״ש, and the heads of the nights, which are כצנ״ש חל״ם — if so, the beginning of the seventh hour of Thursday night belongs to Mars (מאדים). Therefore Moses changed to כחצות, meaning, near midnight, so that the Egyptians would reckon that the plague took place at the end of the sixth hour, under Jupiter (צדק), which is the star of life — and that it would become clear to them that the plague was not from the power of the stars. For had he said to them בחצות ('at midnight'), they would have said that the plague belonged to Mars and was not from YHWH — rather, this was merely imposed upon them by the force of the planetary configuration." "<b>But</b> among the believers it is known that the plague was precisely at midnight, at the very moment Mars began. And thus the Torah testified, for the Holy One, blessed be He, does not change nature except under compulsion and for great necessity, and He wished to use one of His servants, namely Mars, in order to kill His enemies — in the manner of what they said: for the Flood He took one star from Kimah (כימה) and brought a flood upon the world. And even though it would have been a great and public miracle had the plague come at the onset of Mars, even according to the heretics — because those who divine by the stars cannot attribute to Mars the power to annihilate, kill, and destroy except for one individual or two, and that only when it is at its lowest station; but for a multitude of people in a single moment, as happened with the firstborn of Egypt, this is impossible from the power of the constellations — nevertheless Moses did not wish to give an opening even to one of the nimble ones of their number with their vanities, lest they fail to distinguish between individuals and a multitude. He did not want them to say that it happened at the hour of Mars's dominion at all, but rather at the hour of Jupiter's dominion, which is the house of life — for most of those born in the ninth month are under Jupiter. And when death comes to them at the hour of the dominion of the star of life, then it becomes public to them that this is from the power of the Most High, above the stars and constellations. And what you find in the word כחצות ('about midnight') is a support for some commentators, that the end of the sixth hour is near midnight; but the plague in Egypt began precisely at midnight, at that very moment — as it is written (Job 34): ״רגע ימותו וחצות לילה יגועשו עם״ — 'in a moment they die, and at midnight a people are shaken' — in the language of (Psalms 18): ״ותגעש ותרעש הארץ״ — 'and the earth shook and trembled.' ״ויעברו״ — 'and they passed' — these are Israel, for at night He said to them: ״קומו צאו מתוך עמי״ — 'Arise, go out from the midst of my people.' ״ויסירו אביר״ — 'and they removed the mighty one' — not by hand did they remove the dread of Pharaoh and the Egyptians, who were mighty (אבירים) over them, and not by the strength of their hand." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> 'כחצות הלילה' — 'about midnight' — as the verse says (Psalms 119): ״חצות לילה אקום להודות לך״ — 'at midnight I rise to give thanks to You' — ״על משפטי צדקך״ — 'for Your righteous ordinances.' This refers to the night of the plague of the firstborn, which was a judgment upon the Egyptians and righteousness for Israel, to deliver them." "<b>About midnight I go out into the midst of Egypt.</b> Onkelos translated: 'I reveal Myself.' And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: 'Behold, YHWH goes out from His place and comes down,' and so forth — He goes out and comes, measure for measure. Set your heart to understand this."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :register {:flags {1 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:58:10.421213277Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}